<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martin Heidegger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Heidegger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martin Heidegger was a German philosopher from Meßkirch whose work was central to the development of phenomenology, hermeneutics and existentialism. In Being and Time (1927) he introduced the concept of Dasein to describe human existence and sought to reawaken the question of being, and his later work turned to technology, language, art and poetry. In April 1933 he was elected rector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-freiburg">University of Freiburg</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>, remaining a member until 1945. After the war he was banned from teaching, a ban lifted in 1949. His commitment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> remains a subject of scholarly controversy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-heidegger.jpg" alt="Martin Heidegger" width="322" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willy Pragher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freiburg,_Zähringen-_Jahnhalle,_Prof._Martin_Heidegger_während_eines_Vortrags_-_LABW_-_Staatsarchiv_Freiburg_W_134_Nr._023740f_(cropped).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Heidegger was born on 26 September 1889 in rural Meßkirch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, the son of Johanna Kempf and Friedrich Heidegger, the sexton of the village church, and was raised Catholic. In 1903 he began to train for the priesthood and in 1909 entered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> seminary, but was discharged within weeks because of heart trouble. Franz Brentano's thesis on the manifold senses of being in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> first sparked his interest in the question of being. He studied theology and scholastic philosophy at Freiburg, broke off priestly training in 1911 and turned to Edmund Husserl's Logical Investigations. His doctoral thesis on psychologism came in 1913 and a habilitation thesis on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duns-scotus">Duns Scotus</a> two years later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1919 to 1923 he taught at Freiburg as an assistant to Husserl. In 1923 he became extraordinary professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>, where his students included Hans-Georg Gadamer, Karl Löwith, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt">Hannah Arendt</a>, Leo Strauss and Hans Jonas. In 1927 he published Being and Time, which raised him to what is described as a position of international intellectual visibility, and in 1928 he accepted Freiburg's election to succeed Husserl. In the book he analysed Dasein, the kind of being that humans have, through its ordinary &quot;being-in-the-world&quot;, arguing that a prescientific understanding of being precedes logic and theory.</p><p>His later work, which some commentators describe as a &quot;Turn&quot; beginning as early as 1930, concerned the history of being, language and technology. He rejected a sharp about-turn, and scholars such as Thomas Sheehan and Mark Wrathall say the shift is overstated or absent. In his later philosophy he held that &quot;Language is the house of being&quot;, and he turned to poetry for salvation from the language of technology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Heidegger was elected rector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-freiburg">University of Freiburg</a> on 21 April 1933 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> on 1 May. He delivered his Rektoratsrede on 27 May 1933 in a hall decorated with swastikas, with SA members present. In late 1946 the French military authorities barred him from teaching; denazification proceedings ended in March 1949 with his classification as a Mitläufer, and he taught again from the winter of 1950 to 1951. In 1966 he gave an interview to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/der-spiegel">Der Spiegel</a> in which he sought to justify his support of the Nazi Party; it was published after his death on 26 May 1976 in Freiburg. Scholars still debate whether his philosophy is connected with his politics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martin Heidegger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
